you're, you know, at home and people will
install programs.

00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:16.960
And this is how we're seeing major breaches
occur.

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:20.500
Phone, email and SMS have gone through the
roof.

00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:26.510
And now you add to that that Verizon, T-Mobile
and AT&amp;T have all been breached in the last

00:12:26.510 --> 00:12:27.660
12 months.

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:30.520
Your phone numbers out there, my phone number
is out there.

00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:33.840
This is why we're seeing an increase in phone
calls and text messages all over.

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:40.690
Speaker 1: Is there any are there blanket
recommendations that take people out of having

00:12:40.690 --> 00:12:45.480
to make sort of like one on one decisions
as frequently like, for example, I no longer

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:49.811
pick up my phone at all unless I know exactly
who's calling me, and it's for a bunch of

00:12:49.811 --> 00:12:52.820
different reasons, including that people who
don't like me have found my phone number.

00:12:52.820 --> 00:12:56.610
Yeah, but but also because overwhelmingly,
if I don't recognize the number, if it's a

00:12:56.610 --> 00:12:58.670
real call, they're going to leave a voicemail.

00:12:58.670 --> 00:13:01.740
And if it's not a never hear back, so, so
like that would be one thing that I do.

00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:05.500
Are there other blanket things everybody can
just make regular practices?

00:13:05.500 --> 00:13:06.880
Speaker 2: Yeah.

00:13:06.880 --> 00:13:10.040
These will seem a little technical, but I
will say them just because I think that's

00:13:10.040 --> 00:13:11.820
a very important topic.

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:14.570
First is, I encourage everyone to use a password
manager.

00:13:14.570 --> 00:13:15.570
Mm-Hmm.

00:13:15.570 --> 00:13:18.720
Because the issue with the phone calls and
stuff is eventually we're going to answer

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:21.500
a call or we may fall for something.

00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:25.680
But if we use a password manager, then we
don't have the same password on every website.

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:28.300
Yeah, and they're easy to change and control.

00:13:28.300 --> 00:13:33.190
But even if you do that, the next thing is
vitally important, and that's to use multifactor

00:13:33.190 --> 00:13:36.280
authentication on everything that allows you.

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:40.590
And if you have a choice, don't use the one
that sends you a text message using one that

00:13:40.590 --> 00:13:45.720
lets you use an app, you know, like Duo or
Orthe or Microsoft Authenticator or something

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:50.670
like that, because that they need to have
access to your physical phone in order to

00:13:50.670 --> 00:13:51.750
get that.

00:13:51.750 --> 00:13:53.780
And if they have that, you already have bigger
problems, right?

00:13:53.780 --> 00:13:58.520
So those two things alone and there's a ton
of others, but those two things alone could

00:13:58.520 --> 00:14:00.820
save so many people.

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:05.310
And then the third thing I'll say, and this
is something that we can do for free in America,

00:14:05.310 --> 00:14:09.850
which is you could put a freeze on your credit
with all three major credit bureaus.

00:14:09.850 --> 00:14:10.850
Right.

00:14:10.850 --> 00:14:11.850
And you can do that.

00:14:11.850 --> 00:14:12.850
You don't have to pay a service.

00:14:12.850 --> 00:14:13.850
It's easy.

00:14:13.850 --> 00:14:16.640
It takes five minutes and you can unfreeze
it in five minutes.

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:23.170
And I suggest everyone do that because if
you have a password manager, multifactor authentication

00:14:23.170 --> 00:14:27.670
and your your account is locked, people, even
if they steal your data, they're not going

00:14:27.670 --> 00:14:29.120
to be able to use it.

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:33.000
So that keeps you from having to worry that
I just mess up, that I give someone the wrong

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:37.860
thing and it can keep you relative as nothing
will keep you hacker proof.

00:14:37.860 --> 00:14:39.650
But it could keep you relatively safe.

00:14:39.650 --> 00:14:44.690
Speaker 1: Now, in terms of just the incoming
phone calls, is there.

00:14:44.690 --> 00:14:49.520
Sometimes you get legit phone calls because
there really has been, you know, your credit

00:14:49.520 --> 00:14:53.530
card calls because there's a charge they want
to confirm or I don't know if banks really

00:14:53.530 --> 00:14:54.570
call people frequently.

00:14:54.570 --> 00:14:58.840
My bank doesn't call me, but I'm guessing
banks make phone calls, sometimes for real.

00:14:58.840 --> 00:15:05.120
So is there some overarching principle that
can be applied to any incoming phone call

00:15:05.120 --> 00:15:08.570
to prevent falling, even if it's one out of
a thousand that you fall for?

00:15:08.570 --> 00:15:13.130
Speaker 2: Yeah, so I do exactly what you
said, which is I let them go to voicemail

00:15:13.130 --> 00:15:16.270
even if it looks legit or if I do pick it
up.

00:15:16.270 --> 00:15:18.570
And let's just say it's my bank and they go,
Hey, this is your bank.

00:15:18.570 --> 00:15:20.390
We have some weird charges we need to verify.

00:15:20.390 --> 00:15:22.200
Yeah, I say, Hey, I can't talk right now.

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:23.370
I'll call you back.

00:15:23.370 --> 00:15:25.580
I do not call the number on the phone.

00:15:25.580 --> 00:15:27.490
I take my card out of my wallet.

00:15:27.490 --> 00:15:30.930
I call the toll free number and I say, Hey,
someone just called me from the bank saying

00:15:30.930 --> 00:15:33.390
there were some weird charges I need to verify,
right?

00:15:33.390 --> 00:15:37.300
Because I know that I'm talking to the real
bank because I called you.

00:15:37.300 --> 00:15:39.470
But when you call me, I don't know.

00:15:39.470 --> 00:15:43.810
And if they just start trying to verify information,
Heckman, with some of these breaches, they

00:15:43.810 --> 00:15:46.650
have all of that, so they can sound super
legit.

00:15:46.650 --> 00:15:47.650
Sure.

00:15:47.650 --> 00:15:51.000
You know, so I just and don't ever call a
number they give you.

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:56.850
Just yesterday, I had a friend of mine who
got a call, someone saying they're from Amazon,

00:15:56.850 --> 00:16:00.470
and they were helping them try to complete
a purchase on the account.

00:16:00.470 --> 00:16:02.260
And they were they needed them to install
a program.

00:16:02.260 --> 00:16:06.170
They didn't fall for it, but they gave them
a number and he called the number back and

00:16:06.170 --> 00:16:07.560
someone picked on no Amazon.

00:16:07.560 --> 00:16:08.590
How can we help you?

00:16:08.590 --> 00:16:09.590
Right, right.

00:16:09.590 --> 00:16:14.130
Even have people answering the phone with
the, you know, the numbers that they're giving

00:16:14.130 --> 00:16:15.130
out.

00:16:15.130 --> 00:16:16.490
So you can't trust it if they give it to you.

00:16:16.490 --> 00:16:19.420
But if you call a legitimate number, then
you can trust it.

00:16:19.420 --> 00:16:25.970
Speaker 1: Another one I've seen gotten recently
is I got an email saying, Hey, we just charged

00:16:25.970 --> 00:16:30.670
you 40 bucks for the something you ordered
and of course you go.

00:16:30.670 --> 00:16:31.790
I didn't order that.

00:16:31.790 --> 00:16:33.480
I got to go and get a refund.

00:16:33.480 --> 00:16:37.630
So then you look at the info on the email
and of course, if you called, my guess is

00:16:37.630 --> 00:16:40.320
they would say, Oh yeah, what was the credit
card number?

00:16:40.320 --> 00:16:42.390
You put it, and then you're off to the races?

00:16:42.390 --> 00:16:43.390
Yeah.

00:16:43.390 --> 00:16:46.030
Speaker 2: And if you think about that sounds
so legitimate, right?

00:16:46.030 --> 00:16:50.360
Because if I call into an actual vendor, like
if I call my bank right now, they're going

00:16:50.360 --> 00:16:53.340
to say, please verify the account you're calling
about, right, they're not going to give me

00:16:53.340 --> 00:16:54.720
the account.

00:16:54.720 --> 00:16:56.010
They're going to ask me to verify it.

00:16:56.010 --> 00:16:58.770
So I'm going to legitimately give them my
account number.

00:16:58.770 --> 00:16:59.770
Sure.

00:16:59.770 --> 00:17:00.770
Which could be my credit card.

00:17:00.770 --> 00:17:04.310
So if I call what I think is my bank and they
go, Hey, to look you up, we need to verify

00:17:04.310 --> 00:17:05.310
your identity.

00:17:05.310 --> 00:17:07.949
I need your full name, your account number,
your Social Security.

00:17:07.949 --> 00:17:09.490
That sounds all legitimate, right?

00:17:09.490 --> 00:17:11.209
So people will give it over.

00:17:11.209 --> 00:17:15.421
And what they don't know is that if you didn't
call, if you didn't initiate that call, there's

00:17:15.421 --> 00:17:20.679
no way for you to verify that you're speaking
to the actual company, that actual person.

00:17:20.679 --> 00:17:21.679
Right, right.

00:17:21.679 --> 00:17:26.199
So I always say the best practices you have
to initiate the call, you know, unless there's

00:17:26.199 --> 00:17:31.519
some solid proof like, you know, you're standing,
you're you're on the phone and they're verifying

00:17:31.519 --> 00:17:35.620
with you enough that you feel comfortable
with that or they're not asking you for personal

00:17:35.620 --> 00:17:36.620
details.

00:17:36.620 --> 00:17:39.970
I still really just I'd rather just pick up
the phone and call them back.

00:17:39.970 --> 00:17:41.049
Speaker 1: Agreed.

00:17:41.049 --> 00:17:42.049
Agreed.

00:17:42.049 --> 00:17:46.759
We've been speaking with Chris Had Nagai,
founder and CEO of Social Engineer LLC.

00:17:46.759 --> 00:17:51.201
The book is Human Hacking, Win Friends, Influence
People and Leave Them Better Off for having

00:17:51.201 --> 00:17:52.201
met you.

00:17:52.201 --> 00:17:53.201
Chris, always appreciate your time.

00:17:53.201 --> 00:17:53.203
Thanks, David.

